Given a grid of size n*n filled with 0, 1, 2, 3. Check whether there is a path possible from the source to destination. You can traverse up, down, right and left.
The description of cells is as follows:

A value of cell 1 means Source.
A value of cell 2 means Destination.
A value of cell 3 means Blank cell.
A value of cell 0 means Wall (blocked cell which we cannot traverse).
Note: There are only a single source and a single destination.
 

Examples :

Input: grid = {{3,0,3,0,0},{3,0,0,0,3},{3,3,3,3,3},{0,2,3,0,0},{3,0,0,1,3}}
Output: 0
Explanation: The grid is-
3 0 3 0 0 
3 0 0 0 3 
3 3 3 3 3 
0 2 3 0 0 
3 0 0 1 3 
There is no path to reach at (3,1) i,e at destination from (4,3) i,e source.
Input: grid = {{1,3},{3,2}}
Output: 1
Explanation: The grid is-
1 3
3 2
There is a path from (0,0) i,e source to (1,1) i,e destination.
Expected Time Complexity: O(n2)
Expected Auxiliary Space: O(n2)
 

Constraints:
1 ≤ n ≤ 500




class Solution {
    int[] dx = {1,-1,0,0}, dy = {0,0,1,-1};
    boolean dfs(int x,int y,int n,int[][] g,boolean[][] vis){
        if(x<0||y<0||x>=n||y>=n||g[x][y]==0||vis[x][y]) return false;
        if(g[x][y]==2) return true;
        vis[x][y]=true;
        for(int k=0;k<4;k++) if(dfs(x+dx[k],y+dy[k],n,g,vis)) return true;
        return false;
    }
    public boolean is_Possible(int[][] grid){
        int n=grid.length,sx=0,sy=0;
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++) for(int j=0;j<n;j++) if(grid[i][j]==1){sx=i;sy=j;}
        return dfs(sx,sy,n,grid,new boolean[n][n]);
    }
}
